\subsection{Attribution and scope of novelty}

The compact Eq/Q/D logical syntax, Choice-DI rule, logical gates and
selectors, equality reflection invariant, and source-measurement method
are inherited and attributed. The byte leader already contains the
shared major-premise decomposition used here; that refactoring is not
presented as new. The contributions relative to the inspected corpus are
the successor-seeded counter-aware numerical basis, its direct compiler,
and the use of the counter-opening call's argument arity to validate the
successor counter while removing a separate unpacking step.

Neither a literature-wide priority claim nor absolute simplicity is
asserted. A stronger demand that one complete program beat all three
coordinate minima remains unmet. The new theory does satisfy the strict
comparison in normalized bytes, and its numerical equivalence proof is
substantially shorter than the inherited scalar route.

